---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import SignalFlow from '../../components/SignalFlow.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Dual Rig & Dual Amp"
  description="Run two horizontal amp and effect paths and send them to a stereo output."
  lede="Use two rigs when you want two amp sounds at the same time. Ardor shows both paths side by side from the split to the stereo output."
>
  <SignalFlow />

  <h2>Choose the layout</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Use it when you want to</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Dual Amp</strong></td><td>Use two fixed amp and cabinet paths with simple controls.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Dual Rig</strong></td><td>Build a different effect chain on the left and right side.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Dual Amp</h2>
  <p>
    Dual Amp has two horizontal paths. Both paths receive the guitar signal. The left path
    goes to the left output, and the right path goes to the right output. Choose an amp
    model and cabinet sound for each side.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      A Dual Amp block replaces the normal amp and cabinet blocks in that preset. Do not add
      a separate amp or cabinet block to the same preset.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Dual Rig</h2>
  <p>
    Dual Rig gives each side its own horizontal chain. You can use a different amp, cabinet,
    delay, or other effect on each side. Ardor merges the two paths into a stereo output.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Add a Dual Rig block to the main chain.</li>
    <li>Add blocks to the <strong>Left</strong> path.</li>
    <li>Add blocks to the <strong>Right</strong> path.</li>
    <li>Save the preset, then apply it.</li>
  </ol>
  <ul>
    <li>Both paths receive the same mono guitar signal.</li>
    <li>Each path runs from left to right.</li>
    <li>The left path feeds the left output.</li>
    <li>The right path feeds the right output.</li>
    <li>Both paths need at least one block.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The editor marks the layout with <strong>SPLIT</strong>, <strong>LEFT</strong>, <strong>RIGHT</strong>,
    and <strong>JOIN</strong>. This makes the two horizontal paths
    clear while you edit.
  </p>

  <h2>Good uses for two rigs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Send a clean amp to the left and a driven amp to the right.</li>
    <li>Use different cabinet sounds on the two sides.</li>
    <li>Keep one side dry and add delay or reverb to the other side.</li>
    <li>Blend two sounds for a wider stereo part.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Read <a href={url('/docs/signal-chain')}>Signal Chain &amp; Routing</a> for the normal
    left-to-right path, or read <a href={url('/docs/presets')}>Presets &amp; Storage</a> for
    the advanced preset details.
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>Advanced: processing on the Raspberry Pi</summary>
    <p>
      Ardor can process the two paths at the same time on a four-core Raspberry Pi 4. If the
      parallel worker is not available, Ardor processes the paths one after the other. The
      pedal keeps playing in both cases.
    </p>
    <p>
      The release image enables the parallel path with these settings:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Parallel rigs</td><td class="mono">PARALLEL_RIGS=1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Audio CPU</td><td class="mono">AUDIO_CPU=2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rig worker CPU</td><td class="mono">RIG_WORKER_CPU=3</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </details>
</DocsLayout>
